WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.470
- All right, so it's
11:00 am here in New Haven

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.303
in Connecticut.

00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:10.430
I'd like to welcome you all to Yale,

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.000
to this very special event,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.870
a webinar between two distinguished
alums of the department.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.060
I'm actually sitting right in front

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.540
of the Department of Economics,

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.670
where we have not been
located for the past year

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.970
during the Zoom era, but
it's a beautiful spot

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.174
as the Governor knows,

00:00:29.174 --> 00:00:31.150
and as Tavneet knows.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:33.060
It's a beautiful spot,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.920
and I know some of you are
considering coming to Yale,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.880
perhaps as part of our IDE program,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.210
and I hope that you can
experience this spot

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.330
for yourselves next year.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.310
So let me just say that
what we're here for today

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:51.310
is to celebrate the long
history of the Yale IDE program,

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.933
which really was the precursor,

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:58.980
it preceded the creation of
the Economic Growth Center,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.300
both of which have a very
long and storied history

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.083
here in the department,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.340
and I think because of
these two institutions,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.040
the department over the last
50 to 60 years, really 65 years

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.810
has grown into one of the world's centers

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.650
for development economics.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.000
And not only doing
research, but doing policy,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.060
you're asking the biggest questions

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.130
and trying to change the world really

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.090
and answering those policy questions.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.623
How do we develop?

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.217
So we have a long and storied tradition,

00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:31.690
and it's a central part

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.680
of the identity of the
economics department.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.740
It actually goes back to,
I guess, Lloyd Reynolds.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.970
I've read a history that Michael Boozer

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.850
who's the current director
of the master's program wrote

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.820
and so I read some of that
in preparing for this talk,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.653
I learned some things.

00:01:48.653 --> 00:01:50.730
So the history goes
back to Lloyd Reynolds,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.520
who was chair of the department before me.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.170
I'm the current chair, by
the way, of the department.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.510
It goes back to the '50s,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.860
and then he brought in
someone named Robert Triffin

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.900
who created the International

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.500
and Foreign Economic
Administration program,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.740
which Bob Evenson then in the
'60s, renamed to the current

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.260
International Development
Economics program.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.323
So we have a long and storied history.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.310
Today, we have two of our
illustrious grads of the programs.

00:02:24.841 --> 00:02:28.360
One, of either the Yale economics program

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.360
or the IDE program or both.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.670
So we have here Governor Patrick Njoroge.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.710
I hope I'm pronouncing
that roughly correctly,

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.550
'cause I read about it on the web,

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.030
so thank you very much,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.980
who has graciously decided
to agree to join us today.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.493
He received his PhD from
Yale in 1993 if I'm right

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.720
and has worked

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.870
in the International
Economic Policy Community

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.940
for a long time, the IMF and elsewhere,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.217
and is now the Governor of
the Central Bank of Kenya.

00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:04.710
And I should say that
we have a long tradition

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.880
of central bankers coming from
the Yale economics program.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.210
Of course, there's Janet Yellen,

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.810
who's now the secretary of the treasury

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.060
who is got her PhD in '71,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.023
Urjit Patel who won the
Wilbur Cross Medal last year.

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:19.550
A very prestigious medal that we give

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:23.950
to our illustrious grads from
Yale received his PhD in 1990.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.330
I just learned today that the Governor

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.820
of the Bank of Thailand
is also a Yale PhD, 1994.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.620
I won't try to pronounce his name today.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.970
I didn't practice that one.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.310
So, and then Tavneet Suri is also here.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.990
She is not only a Yale
PhD, but a graduate.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.950
One of our illustrious graduates
of the master's program

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.100
in IDE.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.160
And many of our graduates
have gone on to great things

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.630
in policymaking and academia.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:55.520
And Tavneet is one of the role models,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.890
I think for many people.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:01.890
She's worked in many aspects
of research and policymaking

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.170
as a professor at the
Sloan School of Management,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:11.170
but also as a very active
member of the J-PAL,

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.220
the Poverty Action Lab MIT.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.613
So we're very happy to
welcome her here too.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.200
So with those words, let me just say,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.250
I'm looking forward to
today's discussion on FinTech,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.080
which is a very important area

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.260
that Tavneet has worked hard on.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.260
I know, especially, so I
will pass it over to you

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.323
and I look forward to our discussion.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:39.300
- Great, thanks, Tony.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.240
I really appreciate it.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.343
And thanks for the kind words.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.460
Yeah, we do have a storied
history of central bankers,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.420
which is interesting.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.340
Maybe more to come.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.600
So thanks for everyone who's
here, and who's attending.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.320
The way we decided to run this event

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.730
is just have a conversation.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.175
Governor Njoroge is a
pleasure to have you here.

00:05:02.175 --> 00:05:05.510
(speaks in foreign language),
as we would say. (laughs)

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:10.270
And I'm gonna have a
conversation with the Governor,

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.710
and then we'll try and open up to Q&amp;A.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.370
The way the Q&amp;A will work for folks,

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.590
please type in your questions
into the Q&amp;A in Zoom

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:19.940
and I'll moderate and
read them out as we go.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.660
And you can upvote the Q&amp;A
in the Q&amp;A function of Zoom.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.153
So please use that if you have questions,

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.010
and I think we're off to the races.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.160
Governor, welcome again,
thank you for being here

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.860
on behalf of Yale, on behalf of all of us.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.990
It's really nice to start
this series of celebrating

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:42.713
both the Growth Center and
the IDE program at Yale,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.550
sort of kicking off the
celebrations with this event.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.840
And we're really glad you can be here.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:48.713
So welcome.

00:05:49.663 --> 00:05:51.480
- Thank you, Tavneet.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.920
- Yeah, great.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.580
So let me start early days,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.260
and ask you a bit about
kind of your career path,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.720
and how you ended up in
Central Bank Governor in Kenya.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.273
And maybe you can tell us
a bit about that journey

00:06:04.273 --> 00:06:06.770
and how you got there.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:07.980
- Thank you, Tavneet.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.380
And first I'd like to thank, Tony,

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.380
and everyone else in my
alma mater for the invite,

00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:22.240
and I'm definitely delighted
to be back, though virtually.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.240
And thanks, Tony, for that
background on your Zoom portal.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:33.573
And really my journey
started way back when,

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.870
well, as an economist really,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:41.170
when I did my undergraduate and master's

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:42.930
in the University of Nairobi,

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.140
I was quite interested in economics

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.690
more so that I can change the world,

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.030
not so much in the sort of modern way,

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:56.030
but maybe changing the lives
of people in particular ways.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:00.040
So in any event, I did that
and then of course came to Yale

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.140
which was a very exciting time.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.163
We can talk a bit more about that.

00:07:05.707 --> 00:07:10.480
And after doing my PhD,
I went back to Nairobi

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.900
and worked in the government
ministry of finance,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.290
and then eventually decided
that I had seen enough

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.123
of the ministry.

00:07:19.123 --> 00:07:22.970
And I got a job at the IMF from 1995

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:24.690
where I was for 20 years.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.170
That was very exciting.

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.810
I mean, really that is where
I learned to become a real,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.740
I mean, I was nurtured in a sense

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:37.840
and improving my skills et
cetera, 20 years of that.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.840
But I was happy with what I was doing,

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.220
and even career wise, et cetera.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.233
And that's when this job came up,

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.600
the job of the Central Bank Governor.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.640
I wasn't very interested
in it at the beginning

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:59.210
'cause I had everything
sort of in front of me,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.390
but then I had this nagging problem

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:06.370
which is maybe I could make
a difference at home in Kenya

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.480
having worked with all
sorts of other countries.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:14.480
Maybe I could, maybe I won't,
but I could never really tell.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.660
I could not tell unless I tried.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:17.890
So that's what I did.

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:22.890
So I applied and here we
are, 5 1/2 years later

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.720
as the Governor of the
Central Bank of Kenya.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.923
- Great, thank you.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.540
Yes, we'll talk more
about you being Governor

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:33.513
in a little bit.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.227
But let me ask a little bit about the IMF

00:08:37.227 --> 00:08:39.230
and the transition from the IMF

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:41.963
to being Central Bank
Governor to moving back home.

00:08:44.076 --> 00:08:45.700
I spent a lot of time at home,

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.020
but I haven't quite moved
back home yet like you.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.640
So how was that transition,

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.653
and how has it been
over the last few years?

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:57.390
- Yeah, first, I mean I did
enjoy my time at the IMF.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:58.470
It was great.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.500
I was challenged as an economist,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.170
and really honed my
skills as an economist.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:06.670
The transition from sort
of classroom paradigms

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:11.670
to sort of actual policy and
discussion with the authorities

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.340
in governments, across
geographies, et cetera.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:20.180
I mean that I think was
very good training ground,

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:24.570
and I learnt a lot and really
got that sort of instinct

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:27.920
of where things are, et cetera.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.330
So that was very exciting.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.217
Of course we have very interesting people

00:09:31.217 --> 00:09:32.620
that were interested,

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.050
that are still interested

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.590
in development economics generally.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.630
but I think policy was
obviously important.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.600
One of the things that I
learned there of course

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.800
was a phrase that one of the gov--,

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:47.640
I guess somebody told me,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.730
which is no one cares what you know

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.140
until they know how much you care.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.660
And that I think is something
that has stayed with me

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.800
all this time and all these many years.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.780
But the transition coming back home,

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.430
of course you're coming back home

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:08.940
from let's say an environment
where everything is all clear,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:13.443
systems work to a system
where obviously your things

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:18.743
are not working very well.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:22.560
So there was a bit of a transition

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.390
and then realizing that it is you.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:28.680
If you don't do, if you don't
make policy, you don't push,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:29.890
it won't get done.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:32.520
Of course there are other things
that were really surprising

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.370
like of course, I always
knew about Kenya being

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:41.170
at the forefront of digital
money, et cetera, et cetera

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:43.700
but coming back home and realizing that

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.400
to this day, I've never done

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.163
an over-the-counter transaction.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.660
Everything for me is digital.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.910
Yeah, okay, there is an occasional check

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:56.413
that I have to write, but beyond that.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:00.650
So I think it was kind
of exciting in some ways,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:02.870
but also quite challenging in others.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:04.720
Of course, you don't
have much time to worry

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:06.600
about your own personal things.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.140
It's all the issues in front of you,

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.980
but it's been quite challenging,

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:12.390
and I have enjoyed the challenge.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:15.040
- Yeah, and you get to
see family all the time.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.354
- Absolutely.

00:11:16.354 --> 00:11:18.850
(Tavneet laughs)

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.474
And the weather is better
than Washington, I think,

00:11:22.474 --> 00:11:23.447
than also New Haven.

00:11:23.447 --> 00:11:25.620
And so that's something.
- Oh, way better.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.090
Way better. (laughs)

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.464
Yes, one of the things I miss
most, the weather, sadly.

00:11:30.464 --> 00:11:31.614
(laughs)
- Absolutely.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:36.650
- Yeah, I think, it's
interesting, the digital stuff,

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:38.660
it's different seeing it from afar

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:42.210
and then like being sort of in-person

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.340
realizing just how
salient and broad it is,

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.337
and how much it's sort of pervasive

00:11:47.337 --> 00:11:51.780
and a pervasive part of life
around, not just Nairobi,

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.743
but all over.

00:11:54.632 --> 00:11:56.632
So let's talk about that piece a little.

00:11:57.762 --> 00:12:00.130
Why do you think it's been so successful,

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:01.880
and what do you attribute that to?

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.670
Is it kind of the regulation side?

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.640
Do you think there's other
pieces of the success

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:11.160
of this that you would love to talk about?

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.650
- Okay, well, you're right, Tavneet.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:19.113
Really Kenya is the cradle of FinTech.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.090
We could also argue between
ourselves and Ethiopians

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:25.020
whether Kenya is the cradle of man,

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:30.020
but that can take us way into the evening.

00:12:30.359 --> 00:12:31.852
- I think we'll both say the same thing.

00:12:31.852 --> 00:12:32.685
It really is.

00:12:32.685 --> 00:12:34.293
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:12:34.293 --> 00:12:37.300
Two Kenyans talking about this.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:38.770
Yeah, we would agree instantly.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.450
- Exactly. (laughs)

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:42.140
- So the cradle of FinTech,

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.940
and this is really where it started.

00:12:45.424 --> 00:12:49.400
And really that was back in 2006,

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.430
that is when or among other things,

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:56.720
that's when we had the first iPhone.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.640
But I think more importantly,

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:03.640
that's when the product
MPESA came into being,

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.840
actually in 2007,

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.840
that's when we actually
brought it into the live world

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:12.097
as it were.

00:13:12.097 --> 00:13:14.420
And it has been very, very successful.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.120
I think there are many ways of
measuring the success of this

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:19.760
but I think the first one
I need to point out is

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.920
how successful it has been
in bringing us forward

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.250
in terms of financial inclusion.

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:28.930
It was at 26% financial inclusion

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:33.930
or 26% of our adults
were included in 2007.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.710
And now it is actually 83 plus percent.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:43.930
So it has really helped us leapfrog

00:13:45.039 --> 00:13:46.839
in terms of financial inclusion.

00:13:46.839 --> 00:13:49.450
Those are things that I think you and I

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.200
in a policy environment
or class discussion,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.460
you think also of doubling
financial inclusion to 50%.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:57.900
I mean, that's huge.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.440
So going to 85%, and still
increasing is something

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:03.610
that is obviously remarkable.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.080
That's one way of measuring it.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:08.970
But I think your question
is, well, the other ways,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.580
but I'll come to that towards the end.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.240
Why was it successful?

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:14.490
I think first and foremost

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:17.970
because there was a particular need.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.000
So there was a need that it was feeling,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.460
basically it came to us
through the transfer,

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:28.090
payments sort of as a payment platform.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:30.630
So I could send money to people.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.590
There was a need in the
sense that people needed

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:36.740
to send their money to
their family back home

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.340
in the rural areas.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:41.650
And instead of giving it to the,

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:45.950
let's say to the bus conductor
or using the postal service

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.680
and et cetera, et cetera,

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:51.930
I mean this actually filled a very urgent

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.560
and indeed important need.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.190
So, and that's really what happened.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.800
So when it was devised,

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:01.430
it was a really devised
as a transfer between P2P

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.653
as we call it today, between persons.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:05.980
So the need.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:10.310
Secondly, the regulator looked at it,

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:13.760
and actually was innovative.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.950
Meaning didn't want to
throttle the technology,

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:21.370
but actually looked at the
concerns like financial stability

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:24.782
and other concerns, security, et cetera

00:15:24.782 --> 00:15:29.782
and allowed the innovator
to sort of improve

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.770
so as to mitigate against those risks,

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:35.130
and then allow them in a sort of a test

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:38.980
and learn environment
to launch the product.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:41.860
Obviously knowing or understanding

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:43.690
that if there was a significant problem,

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.291
it would be pulled off the wall as it were

00:15:46.291 --> 00:15:47.270
in the manner of speaking.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:51.980
I think that was something
that was also very important

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:54.910
in terms of it.

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:57.410
So your question about regulation,

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:00.450
there were no regulations at the time.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:04.570
So actually the regulations
came later in 2013.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.000
That's when we put some
regulations in place,

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.290
but there was clear understanding
between the innovator

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.190
and ourselves about sort
of the rules of the game

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.260
and how we move that forward.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.770
Obviously at the end of the day,

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:22.530
there was very effective collaboration

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:26.540
amongst everyone else, the
consumers, et cetera, regulators.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:30.840
I mean, among everyone else
who came into this ecosystem.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:34.670
And today, now it is a
very elaborate ecosystem

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.640
that is not just on the payment side,

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:41.640
but has grown into a well, a
mature sort of environment.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:44.330
- Yeah, great, thanks.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:46.400
So let's talk about
that mature environment.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.420
You're just teeing up all
my questions, Governor.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:49.410
I love it.

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:52.770
So one of the big pieces
that has grown from it

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:57.650
is kind of these apps or wallets
that allow you to borrow.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:00.670
So the most successful example in Kenya

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:04.240
is a digital bank account that
the Commercial Bank of Africa

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:07.580
has built over the rails of mobile money.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:10.610
It's called M-Shwari
just for the audience,

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:12.403
not for you, Governor of course.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:17.750
And so that's one of the most successful

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:20.390
digital lending products
and digital bank accounts.

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:23.233
What do you see the future
of those sorts of things?

00:17:24.375 --> 00:17:25.220
Are there benefits to them?

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:26.500
Are there risks?

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:27.800
Are you regulating them?

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.330
How do you think of
those sorts of products

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.600
over the rails of mobile money?

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.333
- Okay, precisely we
do have the rails now,

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.340
and now what we need to
do is to put on the wagons

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:41.200
as it were.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:43.740
And I think one of them is
the one that you mentioned,

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:46.430
or one set of products
are the ones you mentioned

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:47.670
sort of lending.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:51.940
And this is of micro lending.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.940
That's what I guess we
used to call it before.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:57.580
And now on your mobile,
you can call it all sorts

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:00.633
of other things, on your mobile device.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.073
I think there's a great future for them.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.100
And it isn't just that one wagon,

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.230
there are many other wagons
that need to be put in place.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.630
As a matter of fact, with the rails now,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.060
what we need to do is to work to make sure

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.960
that the environment continues to,

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:20.460
or the ecosystem continues to mature.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.640
Of course there are some risks.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.840
And I think these are the concerns

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.280
that we as a regulator
need to watch out for.

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:30.740
It's not our job to innovate

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:34.110
in the sense of saying
this is the direction,

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:36.903
let's say picking winners
in the old fashioned way.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.820
We do understand that was actually a bane

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:43.833
of let's say industrial
policy in the past.

00:18:45.152 --> 00:18:46.440
Industrial policy 1.2.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:51.100
So not picking winners, but
actually setting the rules

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:55.690
so that the innovators can
expand as needed, et cetera.

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:59.310
But I think the concerns often
are, from our perspective,

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:01.040
financial stability,

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:03.730
and this could be if it is
in a lending environment

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:07.850
from over-borrowing or over-lending

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:11.510
or for that matter, the
issues of consumer protection,

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:13.573
so costs, et cetera.

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.870
And these are things that we need to

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:19.200
or the way the consumers
are being dealt with.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:21.620
So that is something
that we as a regulator

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:23.910
need to be not just conscious of,

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.350
but to monitor and improve.

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:31.230
One of the concerns we have
actually is some so-called

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:36.990
well, we call them credit-only lenders

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.520
that are not regulated by the Central Bank

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:41.380
or for that matter by anyone else.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:44.440
And unfortunately they've
grown like mushrooms,

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:47.920
and also like mushrooms,
you need to be careful

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:49.260
which mushrooms you eat.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:51.720
Otherwise you may eat
some poisonous stuff.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:56.720
And this actually have been a
big concern for the consumers.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.090
As a matter of fact,
in terms of their size,

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:04.500
they are less than 1% of
a credit in the system.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.630
Actually they are less
than the smallest bank

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:07.980
that we have here in Kenya.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:12.670
But in terms of the
noise from the consumers,

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:13.910
I mean, it's huge.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:17.320
So we are putting in
place now some regulations

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:22.320
and actually there is a bill
that is in parliament today

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.240
that will put order in this area.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:30.490
But I think the point to
make is that it is important

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:35.280
to be clear from our perspective, CBK,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:39.620
so the Central Bank's philosophy
with regard to regulation

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.350
which is you want to
maximize the opportunities

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:46.670
from innovation while
minimizing the risks.

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:50.363
And the benefits as you say, are huge.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:58.370
One of the first papers I
read about the impact of MPESA

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:00.010
was yours, Tavneet.

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:01.330
You remember that?

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:04.725
Where you mentioned that
the impact of a MPESA

00:21:04.725 --> 00:21:05.558
was what?

00:21:05.558 --> 00:21:06.808
2% of GDP?

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:10.680
- 2% was reduction in poverty, yes.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:15.680
- I never told you that, what
actually went through my mind

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:17.050
at the time.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:21.910
You see, years ago, I was
trying to do a term paper

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.420
at Yale, of course.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:28.800
And T.N. Srinivasan was
the one, it was his class.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.660
And I don't remember what it was about,

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:35.660
but basically we were
discussing the impact of trade.

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.200
Yeah, the trade reforms
that were there at the time.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:44.380
And T.N. was an expert on that.

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:48.460
And how actually in
general equilibrium models,

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:52.610
if you try to measure the
impact of a trade reform

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:55.330
liberalizing trade, the
models always give you

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:57.320
a very small estimate.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:00.080
Somewhere like 1% to 2%.

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:04.767
But we all know that the
advantage of trade reforms

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:08.860
is much more than 2%.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.873
And T.N. told me sort of in
passing as part of my paper.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:17.850
I mean, he made the point that
actually the benefits come

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:21.960
from new products.

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:24.590
And I remember going away
trying to figure out,

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.890
okay, how am I gonna
model these new products?

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.570
Creation destruction
or whatever else it is.

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:36.470
But anyway, the point I'm making
is your 2% as you mentioned

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:38.580
is actually a major under estimate

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.170
because you have all these other things

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:43.300
that have mushroomed being in the wagons.

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.510
And those have actually huge benefit

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:47.410
as we have actually seen.

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.080
- Yeah, absolutely, Governor.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:49.913
I completely agree.

00:22:49.913 --> 00:22:51.930
That's like the pure rails on the rails

00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:53.880
with nothing on the rails, except that.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.520
Yeah, I will say, you mentioned T.N.,

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.430
I took T.N.'s class only a
few years after you, Governor,

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:02.263
but I did.

00:23:02.263 --> 00:23:04.570
It was like a trade development class.

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:06.270
- He was still the same person. (chuckles)

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:09.170
- Yeah, I remember him fondly.

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:12.940
So I can imagine him saying
just what you're saying.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.610
Yeah, I think trying to aggregate
up all the bits and pieces

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:17.660
will take us some while as researchers,

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:20.550
but I think it also means
there's a lot of interesting

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:24.060
open questions to understand
what each of these products

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.680
does and how you can design them better,

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:28.180
how you can build them better.

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:32.710
I personally have been
a little underwhelmed

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:33.650
by the innovation

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:36.610
'cause I feel like there
should be more products.

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:39.870
So I'm hoping there's a lot more coming.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:43.250
And I like your view of regulation being

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:44.850
we don't wanna restrict innovation,

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.387
we just wanna manage the
risk the financial system

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:51.612
that come from it.

00:23:51.612 --> 00:23:56.220
- But Tavneet if you
just let me say one thing

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.210
that just struck me now, (indistinct).

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:02.850
You see I spend a lot of
time listening to innovators.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:05.660
I mean, they come here and
do the dog and pony show

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:08.260
and they show us what they are doing,

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:10.190
their widgets and things like that.

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:12.850
And there are certain
things that strike me.

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:15.800
First, it's a lot of
them that are in Africa

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:18.530
are still stuck in the payments area.

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:20.030
Payments, payments, payments.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.900
Now they all say that they want to reduce

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:23.220
the cost of payments,

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:26.480
but I think they need to expand

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:29.513
and begin to look at
other problems elsewhere

00:24:30.845 --> 00:24:34.676
in the financial space as it were.

00:24:34.676 --> 00:24:38.040
And I think that is what
I would strongly encourage

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:42.273
the innovators if there
innovators on this webinar.

00:24:43.639 --> 00:24:48.300
In the US actually it's not
so much in the payments space.

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:50.760
It's more in the capital markets area,

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:52.280
wealth management, et cetera.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:56.098
That's where there's a lot
more action by FinTech,

00:24:56.098 --> 00:24:56.931
et cetera, et cetera.

00:24:56.931 --> 00:25:00.100
But I think my point here
is let the innovators

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:05.060
look at the problems and figure
out how to fix the problem.

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:08.410
Let them not come with
a sort of a prefixed

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:12.280
or preordained idea that
everything is payments

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:13.350
because that's not true.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.320
I think we need more wagons elsewhere.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:18.020
- Yeah, I'm in complete agreement.

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.280
Sadly, I'm not gonna
disagree with you, Governor.

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:22.190
No controversy here.

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:23.650
- We'll get to a point
where we are gonna disagree,

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:25.360
I'm sure.
- Yeah, I'm sure,

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:26.594
but I agree.

00:25:26.594 --> 00:25:28.660
- It's the first time
we agree on everything.

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:31.530
- I just gave a spiel on a
call this morning, Governor

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:34.420
where I sort of gave this
example, there's not enough wagons

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:38.100
and wagons that work for 70% of people,

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.640
not 5% of people in the country.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.730
I think a lot of people get absorbed

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:45.070
in the Nairobi lifestyle,

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:47.260
kind of that's their view of the country.

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.760
And to be honest until I
did field work in Kenya,

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:53.060
I didn't really appreciate
kind of the breadth

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:54.700
that is our country.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:57.940
So I think that's a piece
where people's use cases

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:00.680
often are for a small group of people.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.600
And then the wagon
doesn't really take off.

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:04.290
It just doesn't do all that much.

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:07.030
So I would also encourage
people to think about

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:10.900
not just wagons, but wagons
that work for large portions

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:14.320
of our population, not just a few.

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:15.410
Awesome.

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:17.360
So we've talked a bit about this stuff.

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:20.270
What do you see as the future of FinTech?

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:21.750
So when we think about innovation,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:25.860
where do you see the
scope for those products?

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.410
What do you think that FinTech itself

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.170
can really bring to the continent?

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:31.220
Bring to our company?

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:37.580
- Okay, my view is that the
future of FinTech in Africa

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:39.330
is bright.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:42.900
I mean, let's begin
there for many reasons.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:46.943
First, there are many needs.

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:51.320
You can look around,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.790
and you'll see many problems
that need to be resolved

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:57.620
in the financial space we
are talking, of course.

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:00.770
And I would call this
sort of more generally

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:04.650
the democratization of financial services.

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:09.030
You and I can have a lot
of less financial services

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:10.700
that are available to us.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:13.660
You can go to even wealth
managers or whatever

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:17.550
or savings options, not
everybody in Africa has that.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:20.600
As a matter of fact, there's a lot of,

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.510
if we want to move ahead
in terms of investments,

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:25.370
savings is a key driver,

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:27.983
but we need to get the
savings from everyone else.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:31.020
So I think there is a lot of need here.

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:34.720
Secondly, you don't
have legacy structures.

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:38.850
So you can actually
leapfrog from where we are

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.640
with very little in terms of structures

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:46.640
to something that is let's say
designed with 21st technology

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:50.293
et cetera, et cetera.

00:27:51.350 --> 00:27:56.270
Sadly, remember 60% of our
population in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:27:56.270 --> 00:27:58.453
are youth.

00:28:00.057 --> 00:28:03.760
And that is about 1/3 of our workforce

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:06.100
is below the age of 25.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.590
So you have a lot of let's say,

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.280
the pyramid is a truly a pyramid.

00:28:12.280 --> 00:28:16.710
And a lot of the youth are
educated, et cetera, et cetera.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:21.040
I mean, it's not being
a PhD sort of person

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:23.740
that you need to, that's
not education here.

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:26.260
As a matter of fact as we have discovered

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:31.260
the Kenyan population are
very technologically savvy.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:34.350
I have stories about this,

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:37.170
and I'm sure we can tell
stories if we more time

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:41.770
about meeting people
who are actually doing,

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:46.054
let's say things that you
and I are kind of not quite

00:28:46.054 --> 00:28:48.310
get there quickly, right?

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:50.331
They have it in their fingertips

00:28:50.331 --> 00:28:54.230
in terms of using technology,
adopting technology.

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:58.170
Our grandmother's kind of very
accustomed to technologies

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.603
that maybe people in the US are not.

00:29:01.510 --> 00:29:04.610
I mean, we're still having
trouble with our TV remote,

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:05.443
et cetera.

00:29:06.372 --> 00:29:09.870
Well, actually people
here are very advanced

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:11.870
in terms of dealing with those things.

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:16.870
But also, as I say, I think
people should begin to look at,

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:22.480
I mean, some of the
things just as examples,

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.980
I know a lot of us are
stuck with the technology.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:29.493
So the questions I get
are blockchain, DLT,

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:31.570
things like that.

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:34.210
I think that's looking
at things the wrong way.

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:35.550
Look at the problem.

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:37.780
What problem are we trying to resolve?

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:41.300
And then ask the question,
what solution do you need?

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.950
So it's a bit like writing a term paper.

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:45.370
You don't write the term paper

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:48.690
by first solving the equations
or whatever else it is.

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:50.380
What is the problem that
you are dealing with?

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.740
What is the economic problem

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:55.850
or the research problem
that you're dealing with?

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:57.333
So it is more that.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:03.420
Having said that, I really
think that applying the,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.703
let's say all the ideas
that are out there,

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:15.780
Africa would really leapfrog,
not just on FinTech,

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:17.730
but in other areas as well.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:20.330
And I think here is
where much more excited

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:24.910
because I mean there's so
much that can change quickly.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:29.910
I talked about more than tripling

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:33.000
of the financial
inclusion numbers in Kenya

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.240
and actually elsewhere in Africa

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:40.240
because of such a sort of
transformative technology.

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:43.490
I mean, you have other things
as well that have changed

00:30:43.490 --> 00:30:44.410
in that way.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:45.760
I mean, in terms of, for instance

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:48.610
and the example that I remember is Rwanda.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.020
Rwanda in terms of ease of doing business

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:56.020
in that much maligned, let's
say, let's just leave it.

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.408
- You shouldn't talk about
ease of doing business anymore.

00:30:59.408 --> 00:31:01.405
- I should. (laughs)

00:31:01.405 --> 00:31:03.248
I mean, that's going to embarrass us

00:31:03.248 --> 00:31:05.270
as the economist as well.

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:08.530
But I think the point is in 2007

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:13.270
there were at number 130
something, 135 or thereabouts.

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:18.270
And by 2017, there were
at number four or five

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:24.240
in terms of doing
business and other things.

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:27.310
I mean, an example that I also in my head

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:33.460
is in 2007, Nigeria was
the second largest importer

00:31:36.590 --> 00:31:39.160
of cement in the world.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:41.600
Number one, of course is the US.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:44.803
But then in 10 years later,

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:47.420
a little more than 10 years later,

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:49.860
actually they were sufficient,

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:53.190
self-sufficient in cement and
actually pushing to export

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:56.760
because there has been such
a change in that industry.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:00.810
So I'm just making the point
that most of the things

00:32:00.810 --> 00:32:03.900
if they are done correctly
can be transformative.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:06.250
Of course, as development experts

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:09.310
or people interested in
development as economists

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:12.360
we ask ourselves, is it actually changing

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:13.961
the lives of people?

00:32:13.961 --> 00:32:16.533
And I think it is, I think it is.

00:32:16.533 --> 00:32:17.882
- Yeah, excellent.

00:32:17.882 --> 00:32:20.450
Yeah, I think what problem
you're trying to solve

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:21.513
is a big one.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:25.060
Especially when people talk
about blockchain as you said.

00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:27.460
I always pushed, like, what is it solving

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:31.085
that a regular pretty decent
database won't solve for me?

00:32:31.085 --> 00:32:32.730
Tell me there's value.

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:36.090
But I think people often
don't quite get that view

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:36.923
of the world,

00:32:36.923 --> 00:32:38.500
which is try and figure out the problem

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:41.250
you're trying to solve for at least,

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:44.470
in my books, also not just
one person, many people.

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:46.780
I want it to be a reasonably broad problem

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:47.930
you're trying to solve.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:51.320
Yeah, I think FinTech will
be interesting to watch

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:52.830
over the next couple of decades.

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.810
I think it will either
take off and be great

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:59.060
or kind of we won't have
figured out enough innovation

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.620
to get as much out of it as we could.

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:02.053
And I hope it's the former.

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:05.390
Let me go back a little bit,

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:10.060
and just talk a bit more
about kind of policymaking

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:12.140
in Africa or Kenya more specifically.

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:14.390
We've known each other for
a few years now, Governor.

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:17.580
I think we met right as you got appointed,

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:21.620
if I remember correctly. (laughs)

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:23.680
And I think it was the museum.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.936
I think that's where we met
with the national museum.

00:33:26.936 --> 00:33:27.769
- Absolutely.

00:33:27.769 --> 00:33:32.480
- Yeah, so, I know you've
worked extremely hard in Kenya

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:35.000
to make our banking system,

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:36.500
should I say more efficient,

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:41.283
let's say more efficient
(laughs) without being explicit.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:44.170
I'd love to hear your perspective

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:47.273
on what you think your biggest
success has been so far.

00:33:49.145 --> 00:33:54.145
And also what you found hard
about your current position.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:57.861
- Wow, and all within
two minutes. (laughs)

00:33:57.861 --> 00:33:58.694
- No, no, take your time.

00:33:58.694 --> 00:34:00.996
We have time, we have time.

00:34:00.996 --> 00:34:02.200
We have plenty of time.

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:05.513
- Okay, well, you see, Tavneet,

00:34:05.513 --> 00:34:10.180
I am not sure at this
point, one can look back

00:34:11.628 --> 00:34:14.393
and sort of be comfortable,
but then let that be.

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:19.118
But if I were to, okay, fine.

00:34:19.118 --> 00:34:22.940
If I'm thinking of the
most significant things,

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:26.630
I think the first one in
my mind without a doubt

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:30.243
would be COVID-19, the
response to COVID-19.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:36.000
This has been a very difficult year

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:37.800
for any policy maker anywhere

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:40.990
and for any economist anywhere.

00:34:40.990 --> 00:34:43.300
And the numbers have
been swinging numbers,

00:34:43.300 --> 00:34:44.400
not so much numbers,

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:49.400
but economies have been
swinging huge numbers.

00:34:49.804 --> 00:34:51.500
I remember in March last year

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:54.540
I was looking at some of my
notes actually from a year ago

00:34:55.640 --> 00:34:58.517
because of we are now at one year there.

00:34:58.517 --> 00:35:01.330
And I was looking at some of
the statements that were made

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:06.330
by the Jay Powell, the
Governor at the Fed, US Fed.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:12.540
And he was saying that we
would have something like

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:16.903
well, actually 30% sort of declines,

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.770
things that nobody ever thought of.

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:26.167
You're thinking huge declines is 5%,

00:35:26.167 --> 00:35:30.043
but it was amazing off
the charts surviving that.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:33.910
But it isn't just surviving,

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:38.610
is actually figuring out
policies that would maintain

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:41.130
and strengthen the economy.

00:35:41.130 --> 00:35:44.900
Even as the containment
measures were put in place,

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:49.133
restrictions on travel, close
down, et cetera, et cetera,

00:35:49.133 --> 00:35:54.133
I think we have been quite
successful all things considered.

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:57.760
And we have had some sort of retrospection

00:35:57.760 --> 00:35:59.210
over the last few weeks.

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:01.660
And in terms of, for instance,

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:04.170
the way the banks have responded

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:05.833
or responded at the beginning,

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:07.993
we've been quite,

00:36:10.249 --> 00:36:11.850
I think we did reasonably well on that.

00:36:11.850 --> 00:36:13.790
The decline actually,

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:15.790
we still haven't seen the first numbers,

00:36:16.892 --> 00:36:18.170
I mean the first print numbers,

00:36:18.170 --> 00:36:21.610
but they estimate
something like 1% growth,

00:36:21.610 --> 00:36:24.403
maybe a little less, but around there.

00:36:25.332 --> 00:36:28.480
Actually, I'm not sure it is just that,

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:29.720
but let's wait a few weeks

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:31.930
and they'll give us the first numbers.

00:36:31.930 --> 00:36:34.620
But I think I told you this, Tavneet.

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:38.570
The first time, I mean,
from around February

00:36:38.570 --> 00:36:41.200
which is when we began
to see this thing coming.

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:44.280
The first time I actually
slept a sound sleep

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:47.670
throughout the night
was sometime in October.

00:36:47.670 --> 00:36:50.960
All those many months sort of figuring,

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:55.870
in a sense, being very very
uncomfortable with things,

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:58.072
et cetera, my mind not at ease.

00:36:58.072 --> 00:37:01.980
So I think that is number one

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:05.060
in terms of what I'm proud of,

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:09.310
and what may be has
been the most important

00:37:09.310 --> 00:37:12.020
if we had not done what we did,

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:14.720
I think we'll be talking a
completely different story.

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:16.823
That's one.

00:37:16.823 --> 00:37:19.390
The second one is maybe
what you mentioned,

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:23.740
but the way I put it is
sort of the strengthening

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:25.233
of the banking sector.

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:28.383
And I think there has been a lot there.

00:37:29.271 --> 00:37:30.104
First and foremost,

00:37:30.104 --> 00:37:32.500
I would say is the
culture that was pervasive

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:33.960
in the banking sector.

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:37.220
A culture that is probably not consistent

00:37:37.220 --> 00:37:42.220
with proper pricing and
ethical practices, et cetera.

00:37:45.530 --> 00:37:47.320
So clean up of the sector,

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:49.320
of course that also meant that we had

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:52.490
to take the hard decision
of closing some banks.

00:37:52.490 --> 00:37:57.070
We've closed three and
put them in receivership.

00:37:57.070 --> 00:38:01.300
But one, we actually
put them in liquidation

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:05.160
and the other two in receivership.

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:10.160
So it was tough obviously
knowing the impact on the people,

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:14.100
and until COVID 19 came along,

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:16.850
this was the toughest
decisions that I ever did

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:18.680
or we ever did.

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:21.490
And we didn't take
those decisions lightly.

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:23.790
I remember one in particular,

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:28.217
we were up 48 hours
straight working on this.

00:38:28.217 --> 00:38:31.630
And that was sort of the final moments

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:35.200
after many weeks and a few months also

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:37.140
sort of trying to deal with those issues.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.400
So that is one.

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:41.160
Now of course, it isn't
enough just to clean up.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.600
You need to put it in play.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:46.860
You need to sort of begin
a new normal as it were.

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:50.715
And I think that is what
we've tried to impact

00:38:50.715 --> 00:38:53.663
all the actors in the sector.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:59.500
And we want the banking sector
to work with, and for Kenyans

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:06.010
and actually during this COVID-19 period,

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:11.730
it is because of that sort
of new found way of looking

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:16.860
at things that we have been
able to do what we we did.

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:21.860
So in that sense, just a
thumbnail sketch on this.

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:25.470
It was more important for Banks.

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:28.600
Banks understood that it is more important

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:31.020
to protect their balance sheet

00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:36.020
as opposed to protecting
their PNL, so their profits.

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:40.160
And in a sense, that's
why they restructured

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:45.160
some of the loans with their
borrowers so as to maintain.

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:50.350
The borrowers of course were selected

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:52.350
in the sense that they were already.

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.670
I mean, they had performing loans.

00:39:55.670 --> 00:39:57.400
So you are not restructuring.

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:58.233
You're not evergreening.

00:39:58.233 --> 00:39:59.470
You are doing the right thing

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.840
which is only performing loans,

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.690
restructuring them to
support them, et cetera.

00:40:03.690 --> 00:40:04.987
So that is important.

00:40:04.987 --> 00:40:07.713
And of course, customer centricity,

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:12.490
correct pricing et cetera, et cetera.

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:13.660
So that's number two.

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:18.150
Number three briefly is strengthening the,

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:20.550
this is something that
most people have not seen

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:24.500
because it's really fixing the backroom,

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:25.763
things in the backend.

00:40:26.630 --> 00:40:29.823
And this sort of strengthening
the financial infrastructure.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:32.713
So the infrastructure itself,

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:36.423
for instance, hardening it to be,

00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:43.100
I mean on cyber issues,
hardening it a lot more.

00:40:43.100 --> 00:40:46.230
Improving for instance the
credit information system

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:52.190
which we have done significant
refresh in that space,

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:53.500
but also other things.

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:56.640
We've improved our RTGS

00:40:56.640 --> 00:40:59.463
which is our real time
gross settlement systems.

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:05.450
And frankly, this now
is at world standard.

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:08.840
And we can now move to as a 24/7 economy

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:10.840
because we have the capacity to do that.

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:14.340
I mean, to run that in terms of numbers,

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:17.060
in terms of all the options that you have.

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:20.030
But importantly, data.

00:41:20.030 --> 00:41:23.170
I mean, if we are in the 21st
century, we have to talk data.

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:25.370
And so we are doing a lot of work

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:30.250
to make sure that we are
putting data together

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:31.780
in a particular way

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:36.780
so that we can make data-based decisions

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:42.050
rather than sort of random
decisions in a sense.

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:43.900
So there's also having

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:48.900
a better central security
depository system.

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:50.490
With all these things,

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:53.200
we will really be in the 21st century.

00:41:53.200 --> 00:41:56.222
And at that point, it
wouldn't be just saying,

00:41:56.222 --> 00:42:00.270
we are an important financial system.

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:03.190
It will be well, we are there.

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:06.780
It is Nairobi because
everything else is in place.

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:09.470
And those are the three
things I could probably say.

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:10.590
- Great, thanks, Governor.

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:11.510
I appreciate it.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:14.661
I'm gonna switch to Q&amp;A in a second.

00:42:14.661 --> 00:42:15.883
But yeah, I think,

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:19.610
it feels like with all of
these improvements now,

00:42:19.610 --> 00:42:22.050
the innovators just
have no excuse anymore.

00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:23.340
Come play.

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:26.294
I think that's what we
need to say to innovators

00:42:26.294 --> 00:42:27.530
is just come play.

00:42:27.530 --> 00:42:28.970
And Nairobi is the
greatest city in the world.

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:29.803
No exceptions.

00:42:30.755 --> 00:42:32.972
- I think that we are
not gonna argue, Tavneet.

00:42:32.972 --> 00:42:34.350
(both laugh)

00:42:34.350 --> 00:42:35.860
We agree entirely.

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:37.180
- Yes.

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:38.520
I argue with lots of people

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:40.010
over Nairobi being the best city though.

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:41.404
I will say.

00:42:41.404 --> 00:42:43.250
All right, I'm gonna start some
questions if you don't mind

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:44.620
from the audience, Governor.

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:46.690
I hope that's okay.

00:42:46.690 --> 00:42:49.280
I'll start with a question
from Irene Wangaki.

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:51.100
Irene, thanks for being here.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:53.800
She asks FinTech credit at the moment

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:55.690
is mostly for consumption smoothing.

00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:59.830
Most SMEs have to go to
banks for sizable funding.

00:42:59.830 --> 00:43:01.643
M-Shwari is not lending to SMEs,

00:43:03.130 --> 00:43:06.550
and there's still a lot of
consumer protection issues there.

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:09.340
Do you have a vision for how FinTechs

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:12.300
can solve SME financing issues?

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:14.650
- Well, excellent,
excellent, excellent, Irene.

00:43:16.078 --> 00:43:19.460
I think this is something
we've been working on

00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:20.720
for some time.

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:24.490
I think we need to dial
back maybe two years.

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:29.490
It was January of 2019
when we went out there

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:33.583
and began looking at the FinTechs.

00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:36.560
Not FinTechs, I'm sorry, SMEs.

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:40.743
And I toured the various
places, the various markets.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:42.983
Actually I loved that.

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:50.760
Since my return, I hadn't had
that sort of freedom to walk

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:53.373
in the thick of things.

00:43:54.918 --> 00:43:58.240
Anyway, but one of the
things that came out of that

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:03.180
actually is a product
that is called Stawi.

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:06.930
And basically this is an
anytime anywhere financing

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:08.733
for SMEs.

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:14.870
And it really, the whole
point here is that as an SME,

00:44:15.510 --> 00:44:18.910
my information in terms
of transactions, et cetera

00:44:18.910 --> 00:44:23.910
that I do, buying selling is on my phone,

00:44:24.710 --> 00:44:27.780
say my mobile platform.

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:32.410
So if that information is
gathered and assessed correctly,

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:35.710
you can actually provide
a pretty good sense

00:44:35.710 --> 00:44:37.960
of a credit score there.

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:40.170
As a matter of fact, I don't
remember the numbers now,

00:44:40.170 --> 00:44:44.040
but it is better than
having a credit officer

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:49.040
in terms of judging the
credit worthiness of somebody

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:51.920
using that information.

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:54.894
So this is something that I think we,

00:44:54.894 --> 00:44:58.550
well, unfortunately COVID-19 came.

00:44:58.550 --> 00:45:03.490
And so that was put on
let's say on the shell,

00:45:03.490 --> 00:45:06.660
on skis, on ice or whatever else it is.

00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:11.040
But I think now that the
economy is beginning to come

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:14.480
or is coming back, this
will be a central point.

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:18.403
Now I should hasten them
to say that the SMEs,

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:23.680
they need credit, but
they need credit plus,

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:26.450
and that plus element, other elements,

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:30.303
like maybe being given some sort of,

00:45:31.530 --> 00:45:35.200
well being trained to
do the accounts better,

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:38.860
or for that matter,
improving their skills.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:40.930
I mean, one of the ones
that we were thinking of,

00:45:40.930 --> 00:45:45.090
and I saw this is (indistinct) welders.

00:45:45.090 --> 00:45:48.260
And they need to be taught
how to do better welding

00:45:48.260 --> 00:45:51.309
sort of joints and things like that.

00:45:51.309 --> 00:45:56.309
And basically they can
do that in three hours.

00:45:56.380 --> 00:45:58.900
So the bank works with them,

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:03.030
and they bring some sort of truck on site

00:46:03.910 --> 00:46:08.490
that actually they taught
how to weld for three hours

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:09.730
in the day there.

00:46:09.730 --> 00:46:12.260
And I think it is those sorts of things,

00:46:12.260 --> 00:46:17.260
innovative ways of let's say
upscaling people's skills,

00:46:18.110 --> 00:46:19.693
providing them with credit.

00:46:21.686 --> 00:46:24.843
One of the person I remember
when I went on this,

00:46:26.730 --> 00:46:31.730
whatever it is, excursion,
was a painter and a carpenter.

00:46:33.610 --> 00:46:38.610
So he would sell paint to
the people that are painting.

00:46:39.710 --> 00:46:42.160
They repair your car, and
then they have to repaint it.

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:44.544
So his job was to sell.

00:46:44.544 --> 00:46:46.773
It was all about paint.

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:50.720
And we asked him, so what will it take

00:46:50.720 --> 00:46:53.890
for you to move to the next level?

00:46:53.890 --> 00:46:58.010
And he said, well, actually all
it will take me is something

00:46:58.010 --> 00:47:03.010
like a $1,000 worth of equipment

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:06.070
which would make him sort of,

00:47:06.070 --> 00:47:09.080
or allow him to sort of mix the paints,

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.381
and therefore have all sorts of colors.

00:47:11.381 --> 00:47:14.820
Not just have the, I don't
know, the 10 different colors

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:18.360
or however many colors you need
in that sort of environment.

00:47:18.360 --> 00:47:22.080
So I'm just making the point
that the SME story is a big one

00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:26.100
and we need to work on
it in all directions.

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:30.110
We just started recently
a credit guarantee scheme,

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:33.083
but credit is not everything,
it is credit plus.

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:35.130
- Great, thanks, Governor.

00:47:35.130 --> 00:47:39.430
I've got a question from Ali
O. Paul who starts off saying

00:47:39.430 --> 00:47:41.290
Kenya's work on FinTech
is truly impressive.

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:42.560
Thanks, Ali.

00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:45.430
The Governor will take full
credit for it, don't worry.

00:47:45.430 --> 00:47:47.270
He asks being the cradle of FinTech,

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:49.307
he's curious about what
lessons you might have

00:47:49.307 --> 00:47:51.730
for Central Bank policy

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:54.274
of having a mature FinTech environment.

00:47:54.274 --> 00:47:57.672
You touch on the
financial stability angle.

00:47:57.672 --> 00:47:59.740
Are there any specific
lessons for the transmission

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:02.203
of monetary policy that you think about?

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:05.304
- Wow, difficult question.

00:48:05.304 --> 00:48:06.920
(laughs)

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.990
I mean, generally, I think,
depending on the wagons

00:48:09.990 --> 00:48:14.000
that you put there, you'll have different,

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:18.010
let's say risks on transmission
on monetary policy.

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:20.150
Obviously, if it is lending,

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:22.730
then this has a significant impact.

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:24.570
And we've had to do some work.

00:48:24.570 --> 00:48:26.980
As a matter of fact, for
you to know we are improving

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:28.853
our monetary policy framework.

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:32.870
Precisely because of reasons such as this

00:48:32.870 --> 00:48:37.223
because now mobile
money is very important.

00:48:39.438 --> 00:48:40.340
It is no longer just something

00:48:40.340 --> 00:48:45.330
that is in the corner
somewhere lending, et cetera.

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:48.063
And therefore you need to incorporate that

00:48:48.063 --> 00:48:51.693
when you are thinking
or designing a policy.

00:48:52.556 --> 00:48:53.389
So there is that.

00:48:53.389 --> 00:48:55.660
But I think that is only one angle.

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:58.417
The other elements, depending
on the wagons, as I say,

00:48:58.417 --> 00:49:00.670
if you are into savings,

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:02.540
by the way, remind me, Tavneet,

00:49:02.540 --> 00:49:04.693
to tell them about our savings product.

00:49:04.693 --> 00:49:05.526
- Yes.

00:49:05.526 --> 00:49:07.220
- If you're into savings product,

00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:10.620
then obviously transmission
will be somewhat different,

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:12.410
but it will still be there.

00:49:12.410 --> 00:49:17.410
My point is, you need to
think through it step by step.

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:21.390
And so there's no shortcut.

00:49:21.390 --> 00:49:23.580
You have to do the sort of the brute force

00:49:23.580 --> 00:49:28.580
sort of step by step checking
of how this monetary policy

00:49:30.530 --> 00:49:33.660
is being transmitted
through all the channels

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:36.570
and indeed through all the
products that are there.

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:39.313
But in terms of Central
Bank policy more generally,

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:42.650
I don't think the biggest issue for me

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:45.540
for this product is on the risk.

00:49:45.540 --> 00:49:47.360
I don't think it is on monetary policy.

00:49:47.360 --> 00:49:50.880
I think the biggest risk is
more on financial stability.

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:53.400
And that is an important element.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:57.500
And I think here is where
you need to be more on top

00:49:57.500 --> 00:49:59.593
in terms of monitoring.

00:50:00.630 --> 00:50:04.543
Monitoring, getting data
is so very important.

00:50:04.543 --> 00:50:08.090
This is one of the reasons
why we are much further ahead

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:11.000
on data and we want to make sure

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:14.210
that we have sort of a data lake

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:17.003
that you can slice and
dice any whichever way.

00:50:18.525 --> 00:50:21.570
And I think in that sense,
then you can see the problem

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:25.960
before it comes over
the horizon as it were.

00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:28.180
You can already begin
to the elements of that.

00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:29.600
So that is there.

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:33.000
And then the other thing,
the last point on this is

00:50:34.235 --> 00:50:37.180
the law needs to change.

00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:40.240
I mean, a lot of us
think that you have a law

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:41.680
and it is static.

00:50:41.680 --> 00:50:44.610
Actually, in this area
because it's very dynamic,

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:47.993
the law needs to be much more malleable.

00:50:49.030 --> 00:50:54.030
And so how you craft it,
the way you write a law,

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:57.290
well, that that's one issue.

00:50:57.290 --> 00:50:59.200
But I think also appreciating

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:04.200
that you do need to go to the
law and amend it and tweak it

00:51:04.420 --> 00:51:05.750
in whichever way,

00:51:05.750 --> 00:51:09.140
and maybe it is in the law
directly, it's also other things

00:51:09.140 --> 00:51:12.290
like the Prudential
guidelines when they come out

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:14.880
you need to, I mean, writing this quickly

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:16.430
and change them as needed.

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:20.940
So those are the directions
I would probably put out for,

00:51:20.940 --> 00:51:23.583
I mean, to think in terms
of Central Bank policy.

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:27.020
- Yeah, to lead to one of
the questions in the chat.

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:28.430
I'm not gonna ask the question directly,

00:51:28.430 --> 00:51:32.040
but it's about kind of, how
do you think about big data

00:51:32.040 --> 00:51:34.080
that's also coming from the telcos,

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:38.680
and is there a piece of that
that gets regulated by you guys

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:41.510
for use in credit scoring,
et cetera, like you said,

00:51:41.510 --> 00:51:43.800
and how do you think of that data piece?

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:45.120
I know we have a data bill

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:51.110
that imposes significant
restrictions on the use of data,

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:52.430
but we're going into a world

00:51:52.430 --> 00:51:56.120
where you know a lot about
people from their phones,

00:51:56.120 --> 00:52:00.190
and as people use smartphones,
you learn even more.

00:52:00.190 --> 00:52:01.960
The digital lending apps can see Facebook,

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:05.747
can see your car records, can
see your text message content.

00:52:05.747 --> 00:52:06.580
So we're getting to the point

00:52:06.580 --> 00:52:09.430
where some people have
access to a lot of datas.

00:52:09.430 --> 00:52:12.970
Do you think of how you regulate them

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:16.550
or is that up to the
communications commission,

00:52:16.550 --> 00:52:18.490
and how do you see that
regulation process?

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:22.060
Because it fits into the
credit banking system somewhat.

00:52:22.060 --> 00:52:23.050
- Yeah, a good question.

00:52:23.050 --> 00:52:26.230
I think we are definitely involved in it.

00:52:26.230 --> 00:52:27.070
Let's start there.

00:52:27.070 --> 00:52:30.540
But I think it isn't so
much who is involved in it

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:32.120
that is important.

00:52:32.120 --> 00:52:34.700
I think it is the attitude with which

00:52:34.700 --> 00:52:38.360
or the prism that you view
the data that is available

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:39.320
wherever it is.

00:52:39.320 --> 00:52:40.700
I think first and foremost,

00:52:40.700 --> 00:52:42.863
the data belongs to the consumer.

00:52:44.679 --> 00:52:45.770
It does not belong to the telco.

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:50.770
It does not belong to
the, let's say the bank

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:55.900
that is working with the individual telco

00:52:56.210 --> 00:52:57.870
or whatever else it is.

00:52:57.870 --> 00:53:01.260
And therefore to use the data,

00:53:01.260 --> 00:53:04.853
you do need to provide them with,

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:09.890
I mean, you need to do it
with a complete understanding

00:53:09.890 --> 00:53:12.130
of the consumer.

00:53:12.130 --> 00:53:17.130
And here is where I think,
and sorry to put it this way,

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:19.173
but I think we all understand it.

00:53:20.477 --> 00:53:23.640
Your iPhone or whatever, it updates

00:53:24.550 --> 00:53:26.910
to a new operating system, right?

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:28.100
13 points, whatever.

00:53:28.100 --> 00:53:30.759
I don't know now it's
13 or whatever it is.

00:53:30.759 --> 00:53:32.578
- I don't actually know either. (laughs)

00:53:32.578 --> 00:53:33.730
- Whatever it is.

00:53:33.730 --> 00:53:35.810
This is exactly my point.

00:53:35.810 --> 00:53:39.080
So for me, at least it updates.

00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:42.710
I leave it in the evening,
that's when it'll update.

00:53:42.710 --> 00:53:45.433
And then in the morning, it'll ask me,

00:53:48.157 --> 00:53:52.130
push this to agree on the
conditions, and yes or no.

00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:55.440
I mean, you know so well,

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:57.550
and they know that I cannot say no.

00:53:57.550 --> 00:53:59.730
I just push yes, and move on.

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:01.500
It's not informed.

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:04.273
So this informed consent
is very important.

00:54:05.590 --> 00:54:09.660
It has to be brought down to
the level of the consumer.

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:14.660
And also the consumer needs
to be given many opportunities

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:18.780
to in a sense withdraw from
all the sort of engagement

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:23.480
and all the data is
removed and it's hard work.

00:54:23.480 --> 00:54:24.970
Surely, it's hard work.

00:54:24.970 --> 00:54:28.920
But it is important to do all there is

00:54:28.920 --> 00:54:30.280
to protect the consumer.

00:54:30.280 --> 00:54:31.580
I think what has happened now,

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:34.280
and this is the problem of
some of the digital lenders

00:54:35.370 --> 00:54:36.780
that we are dealing with

00:54:36.780 --> 00:54:40.620
is when you join, they will ask you,

00:54:40.620 --> 00:54:44.680
we would like to use
your list of contacts.

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:49.220
And then you say, yes,
and you move on, right?

00:54:49.220 --> 00:54:50.344
- Yeah.

00:54:50.344 --> 00:54:51.800
- And then what happens?

00:54:51.800 --> 00:54:54.540
They haven't told you how
they are gonna use it.

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:58.450
But what happens is when you don't repay,

00:54:58.450 --> 00:55:02.230
they will call people
on your contacts list.

00:55:02.230 --> 00:55:03.600
I mean, just think of that.

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:06.923
Your family, your boss,

00:55:08.557 --> 00:55:11.840
and your priest back in the rural area.

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:15.860
And everybody's been told,
listen, so-and-so hasn't

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:18.160
and this is your brother.

00:55:18.160 --> 00:55:19.810
He hasn't paid up.

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:21.200
You need to do something.

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:22.790
So I'm just making the point that

00:55:22.790 --> 00:55:24.753
that is not informed consent.

00:55:26.405 --> 00:55:28.570
- I use that example a lot saying,

00:55:28.570 --> 00:55:30.860
also, I think, Governor, the difference is

00:55:30.860 --> 00:55:32.440
if Apple had something really bad

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:35.756
in the terms and conditions,
someone would have found it

00:55:35.756 --> 00:55:38.200
and there would have been a process

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:40.240
around uproar to solve it.

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:42.800
That's less so in this case, I think.

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:45.997
The institutions don't
function quite the same way.

00:55:47.132 --> 00:55:48.380
- But Tavneet, here, I disagree with you.

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:49.510
I don't think that-

00:55:49.510 --> 00:55:51.503
- Yay, something you disagree with me on.

00:55:51.503 --> 00:55:52.336
(Patrick laughs)

00:55:52.336 --> 00:55:54.540
- I mean, there was this,

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:56.450
anyway, it's on my phone somewhere,

00:55:56.450 --> 00:55:58.132
would have read it to you,

00:55:58.132 --> 00:56:00.900
but when they introduced
something, when was it?

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:02.690
Back in 2019.

00:56:02.690 --> 00:56:07.100
And in a sense it was, anyway,
it wasn't informed consent.

00:56:07.100 --> 00:56:08.323
Let's just be honest.

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:11.890
- Yeah, I think this
is gonna be a big issue

00:56:11.890 --> 00:56:15.370
is how we think about who owns data,

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:18.960
and how you give consent to
use that data and for what,

00:56:18.960 --> 00:56:21.700
and as we do digital
identification as well

00:56:21.700 --> 00:56:23.380
with the Huduma number
and things like that

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:24.830
and start to connect it to things,

00:56:24.830 --> 00:56:27.080
I think this is gonna
become really important

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:32.910
from like you said a consumer
information perspective,

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:34.963
but also a regulatory perspective.

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:38.580
- And I think that's the,

00:56:38.580 --> 00:56:41.233
to be honest this is where I have.

00:56:42.680 --> 00:56:46.023
Well, we've been a bit concerned
about the large telcos.

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:50.490
If I may just have a segue into that,

00:56:50.490 --> 00:56:55.430
because in a sense there is a
bit of a monopolistic behavior

00:56:56.530 --> 00:57:00.580
as they try and sort of grab data.

00:57:00.580 --> 00:57:03.578
There's a lot of data
in the payment space,

00:57:03.578 --> 00:57:06.120
a lot of data in the
payments space about people,

00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:07.490
et cetera, et cetera.

00:57:07.490 --> 00:57:09.680
And so when you get a large telco

00:57:09.680 --> 00:57:11.520
getting into the payments space,

00:57:11.520 --> 00:57:13.060
and not just a large telco,

00:57:13.060 --> 00:57:16.513
I'm just talking about
the big tech generally,

00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:18.910
we need to be careful.

00:57:18.910 --> 00:57:22.620
I mean, and this is one of
the things that in my mind

00:57:22.620 --> 00:57:27.620
when you think of some
of the large big techs

00:57:28.410 --> 00:57:32.750
around the world sort of
making inroads into Africa,

00:57:32.750 --> 00:57:34.160
I think the first question is

00:57:34.160 --> 00:57:36.190
what are they gonna do with the data?

00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:38.820
And I think in some sense they also know

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:41.640
that there's a first mover advantage.

00:57:41.640 --> 00:57:44.560
So once they grab some of this data,

00:57:44.560 --> 00:57:48.493
it would be very difficult
to get it out of their hands.

00:57:50.351 --> 00:57:52.730
And so I think we need
to be careful about data.

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:56.010
We have a responsibility as regulators

00:57:57.282 --> 00:58:01.670
to make sure that the people
that we are responsible for

00:58:01.670 --> 00:58:06.670
do not fall in those sort of
problems in our jurisdictions.

00:58:09.580 --> 00:58:10.727
- Great.

00:58:10.727 --> 00:58:13.020
I think we're close to
running out of time.

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:17.300
So I think we should wrap up.

00:58:17.300 --> 00:58:19.630
There is just two minutes left, Governor.

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:23.170
So let me take the
opportunity to thank everybody

00:58:23.170 --> 00:58:25.000
who's attended and thank
you for being here.

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:27.083
Sorry, we didn't get to all the Q&amp;A,

00:58:27.930 --> 00:58:30.430
but I really wanna thank you, Governor,

00:58:30.430 --> 00:58:33.690
for being with us today and
kicking off our celebrations

00:58:33.690 --> 00:58:37.280
of the Growth Center and the
IDE program, and making time.

00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:38.780
I know it's evening in Nairobi

00:58:38.780 --> 00:58:42.190
and just having a conversation
with us which is way more fun

00:58:42.190 --> 00:58:44.010
than speaking, I think.

00:58:44.010 --> 00:58:46.460
And so for, at least for me, it is.

00:58:46.460 --> 00:58:47.760
I hope it was for you too,

00:58:47.760 --> 00:58:50.170
but we're really grateful for your time,

00:58:50.170 --> 00:58:52.750
and for you being here today.

00:58:52.750 --> 00:58:55.053
I don't know, Tony, if
you wanna add anything.

00:58:56.460 --> 00:58:57.383
- Thank you, Tavneet.

00:58:58.850 --> 00:58:59.683
- That was great.

00:58:59.683 --> 00:59:00.516
Really interesting.

00:59:00.516 --> 00:59:03.590
The Q&amp;A format was quite useful.

00:59:03.590 --> 00:59:05.450
And I learned a bunch
of things I didn't know

00:59:05.450 --> 00:59:08.320
about how money and
finances work in Kenya.

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:10.160
I knew a few things, but
it's quite interesting,

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:12.455
and you're kind of on the forefront there.

00:59:12.455 --> 00:59:13.450
I think we can learn a lot from that

00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:14.970
from what's going on there.

00:59:14.970 --> 00:59:18.600
So I think it was a great event,

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:22.360
very much enjoyed you both participating.

00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:24.421
And I'll just close with saying,

00:59:24.421 --> 00:59:29.421
this just shows the extent to
which I think Yale is a center

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.540
for development economics.

00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:34.020
So this kind of
conversation happening here

00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:35.420
is an illustration of that.

00:59:35.420 --> 00:59:40.400
And let me congratulate
again the IDE program

00:59:40.400 --> 00:59:42.070
on 65 years of existence.

00:59:42.070 --> 00:59:44.970
It's one of the premier programs
of its kind in the world.

00:59:45.935 --> 00:59:48.540
So that was great in
showcasing all that success

00:59:48.540 --> 00:59:50.050
with that program.

00:59:50.050 --> 00:59:51.315
So thank you for participating.

00:59:51.315 --> 00:59:52.415
- Thank you, Tony.

00:59:52.415 --> 00:59:53.828
- Thank you, Tony.

00:59:53.828 --> 00:59:54.661
Thank you, Tavneet.

00:59:54.661 --> 00:59:56.453
- Hopefully see you in Nairobi soon.

00:59:57.645 --> 00:59:59.213
- We hope so.

01:00:00.270 --> 01:00:01.950
Looking forward to seeing you.

01:00:01.950 --> 01:00:03.448
- Yes, sir.

01:00:03.448 --> 01:00:04.820
Thank you for being here,
we really appreciate it.

01:00:04.820 --> 01:00:06.220
- Okay, all the best.

01:00:06.220 --> 01:00:08.440
Goodbye to the participants.

01:00:08.440 --> 01:00:10.463
- [Tavneet] Thank you.
- [Tony] Thank you.

